  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: As high pressure builds over the PNW and \ndrier air moves in, temperatures will continue to be on the slight \nuptick and minimum relative humidity will continue to decrease. This \nwill limit thunderstorm chances to only small mountain areas this \nafternoon and evening. Lingering moisture in the far northern \nCascades and the Blue Mountains combined daytime heating will result \nin a 5 to 10 percent chance of isolated thunderstorms. Main threats \nwith these thunderstorms include gusty outflow winds up to 30 mph, \nlightning induced fire starts, and heavy downpours. These \nchances decrease to 5 percent tomorrow. \n\nOf more importance tomorrow is the increasing onshore flow combined \nwith a cross-Cascade pressure gradient that will bring breezy to \ngusty winds to the Cascade gaps and lower Basin areas. With minimum \nRHs in the low 20 percent range, the primary concern tomorrow will \nbe elevated fire weather conditions. Current projected wind gusts \nare 15-20 mph with localized wind gusts up to 25 mph in some places. \nThese values and ranges are borderline for fire weather headlines, \nbut minor impacts still exist and precautions should still be taken \nto avoid any new fire starts. \n\nBy Wednesday, winds will decrease and temperatures will remain in \nthe mid to high 80s. Due to increasingly dry conditions, there is \na near zero chance for precipitation from midweek on. \n\nThursday through Saturday: As the ridge stays in place over the \nforecast area, temperatures increase on Thursday and Friday into the \nlow to mid 90s. This will result in Moderate HeatRisk across much of \neastern Washington on Thursday and Friday. Moderate HeatRisk impacts \nall those sensitive to heat and do not have access to adequate \ncooling and hydration, alongside beginning to impact some health \nsystems and industries. \n\nOn Friday, the low pressure system will begin moving eastward \ntowards the PNW coast. By Saturday, the upper level trough will move \nthrough the state of Washington, ushering in a cold front. This cold \nfront will bring widespread gusty winds to the Inland Northwest. \nThere is at least a 30 percent chance and above for most of the \nBasin, Waterville Plateau, and Palouse areas to see wind gusts 30 \nmph or higher. Those chances increase to 50% and above as you move \nwestward into the Waterville Plateau. Hot, Dry, Windy Index values \nfor this day are nearing the 90th percentile for most areas \nmentioned above. Models are also showing increasing confidence \nin thunderstorms ahead of the frontal passage. Nearly 75% of all\nclusters support this pattern setup, indicating increasing \nconfidence in Saturday seeing elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions. /AS",
